    "question": "Can you explain how the rain shadow effect works and how it influences regional climates?",
    "answer": "The rain shadow effect basically happens when you have a mountain range. When wet air blows toward the mountains, it gets pushed up and cools down, causing a lot of rain on that first side. But by the time the air gets over to the other side, it has lost most of its moisture. So, that far side ends up being really dry, often forming a desert. That is why you get such different climates next to each other.",
    "domain": "geography",
